4.97.m:
Their liṅgas also exist there by the thousands;
from seeing them, men attain the realm (salokatā) of each respective attendant.
To the west of Nandīśvara is Śilādeśa, which removes evil thoughts;
there is the auspicious Hiraṇyākṣeśvara, who bestows great strength.
To the south of it is the liṅga named Aṭṭahāsa, bestowing all happiness;
to its north is the auspicious liṅga named Prasannavadaneśa.
From the auspicious vision of it, a devotee remains serene-faced (prasannavadana);
to its north is the Prasannoda pool, which grants purity to men.
To the west of Aṭṭahāsa are the two [liṅgas] named Mitra and Varuṇa,
which bestow their world, are worthy of worship, and destroy grave sins.
To the southwest of Aṭṭahāsa is the liṅga
named Vṛddhavāsiṣṭha; from worshiping it, great knowledge arises in men.
Situated near Vasiṣṭheśa is Kṛṣṇeśa, bestower of the world of Viṣṇu;
to its south is Yājñavalkyeśa, increaser of the radiance of Brahman.
Having worshiped Prahlādeśvara, which increases devotion, to its west

[is the place] where Śiva merged of his own accord, desiring to grace his devotees.
Therefore, the Svalīna liṅga to the east of that is to be worshiped with effort.
For those who are always devoted to knowledge and desire supreme bliss,
the state that is prescribed for them, is [attained] at Svalīna by those who cast off the body.
The Vairocaneśvara liṅga is situated in front of Svalīna;
to its north is Balīśa, which greatly increases great strength.
Right there is the Bāṇeśa liṅga, worshiped, fulfilling all desires;
to the east of Candreśvara is the liṅga named Vidyeśvara.
All lores become propitious through service to that liṅga;
to its south is Vīreśa, bestower of great perfection.
Right there is the goddess Vikaṭā, who frees from the flood of all sorrows;
that is known as the great seat (pīṭha) of five seals (pañcamudra), bestowing all perfections.
Great mantras chanted there are quickly accomplished, not otherwise;
in the northwest corner of that seat, Sagareśvara is to be fully worshiped.
From worshiping it, the fruit of a horse-sacrifice is complete;

To the west of Rudrakuṇḍa is the liṅga Bṛhaspatīśvara;
having seen it on the conjunction of Guru and Puṣya, one obtains divine speech.
To the south of Rudrāvāsa is the excellent liṅga Kāmeśa;
to its south is a great pool granting the desired wish through bathing.
A pilgrimage there on the thirteenth day of the bright fortnight of Caitra grants desires.
To the east of Kāmeśvara is the auspicious Nalakūbara liṅga;
in front of it is a purifying well, bestowing abundance of wealth and grain.
To the east of Nalakūbara are Sūryeśvara and Candramaseśvara;
when properly worshipped, the two remove the veil of darkness that is ignorance.
To its south is Adhvakeśa, who, when beheld, destroys delusion.
There is the liṅga Siddhīśvara, granter of great perfections;
and right there is Maṇḍaleśa, who bestows the status of a sovereign ruler.
To the east of Kāmakuṇḍa is Cyavaneśa, bestowing prosperity;
right there is Sanakeśa, granting the fruit of a Rājasūya sacrifice.
To the west of that is the Sanatkumāra liṅga, producer of perfection in yoga;
to its north is Sanandeśa, who enables great knowledge.

To its south is Āhutīśa, who, when seen, grants the fruit of fire offerings;
to its south is the merit-generating liṅga Pañcaśikheśvara.
To its west is the Mārkaṇḍeya pool, which increases merit;
having bathed in that pool, why should a man grieve any longer?
Bathing and charity performed there yield imperishable merit;
to its north is Kuṇḍeśa, bowed to by all perfected beings.
Whatever fruit is obtained after twelve years upon receiving the Pāśupata initiation,
that fruit a mortal obtains, O brahmin, from the darśana of Kuṇḍeśa.
East of the Mārkaṇḍeya pool is Śāṇḍilyeśa, granting great merit;
to its west is Caṇḍeśa, who destroys the sin [incurred during] an eclipse of the hot-rayed sun.
To the south of Kapāleśa is a pool called Śrīkaṇṭha;
a man who has bathed in that pool becomes a donor through the power of fortune.
Near that pool is the liṅga Mahālakṣmīśvara;
having worshipped Mahālakṣmī and bathed in the waters of that pool,
one is fanned by celestial maidens holding chowries in their hands.

a man who quickly beholds him shall dwell in the city of the Three-Eyed One for a kalpa. // 85
To the south of Gabhastīśa, however, is Maṅgalā, the abode of auspiciousness;
having dedicated it to Maṅgalā Gaurī, one should feed a married brahmin couple // 86
after ornamenting them according to one's means; there is never an end to that merit.
A single circumambulation of Maṅgalā bears the fruit of circumambulating the earth. // 87
North of Mukhaprekṣeśvara is the goddess Vadanaprekṣaṇā;
near Maṅgalā, she is Śivā, the bestower of all supernatural powers. // 88
North of Mukhaprekṣa are the two auspicious liṅgas, Tvaṣṭrīśa and Vṛtteśa;
from beholding the two comes the fruit of donating land along with gold. // 89
North of that, seeing the goddess Carcikā is auspicious;
and in front of Carcikā is the Revateśvara liṅga, which produces peace. // 4.2.97.190
In front of the great auspicious temple is the Pañcanadeśvara liṅga;
west of Maṅgalā is the auspicious great well Maṅgaloda. // 91
West of Maṅgalā is Upamanyu's auspicious great liṅga;
west of that is the Vyāghrapādeśvara liṅga, which removes fear of tigers. // 92

To the southwest of Gabhastīśa is Śaśāṅkeśa, which removes heaps of sins;
west of that is the Caitraratha liṅga, which bestows a divine realm. // 93
West of Revateśa is Jaiminīśa, which removes great sins;
there are many liṅgas of the seers there, O best of seers! // 94
To the northwest of Jaiminīśa is indeed the Rāvaṇeśvara liṅga;
from beholding it, men have no great fear of rākṣasas. // 95
To its south is Varāheśa, then to the south Māṇḍavyeśa;
to its south is Pracaṇḍeśa, and to the south of that is Yogeśa. // 96
To its south is Dhāteśa, and in front of it is Someśa;
to its southwest is Kanakeśa, who gives abundant gold to the good. // 97
North of that are the five liṅgas of the Pāṇḍavas, for the joy of the good;
in front of that is Saṃvarteśa, and to its west is Śveteśa. // 98
West of that is Kalaśeśa, a liṅga that grants freedom from the fear of Death;
when Śveta was ensnared by Death, O sage, it arose from the water pot. // 99
North of that is the Citragupteśvara liṅga, which removes sins;
west of Citragupteśvara is Dṛḍheśa, yielding great fruit. // 4.2.97.200
